00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.190
CHRIS BOEBEL: So welcome
to the second lab,

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:28.180
which as we promised,
will primarily

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.490
focus on audio recording.

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:33.160
Both the specific
technical aspects

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.050
of setting up our
audio package, what's

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:39.100
in it, what it can do and also
some general tips on getting

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.240
good recordings.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.460
Again, this is not a class
in digital signal processing

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.970
or very intense field recording.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:52.300
We're really going to
focus on the basics of what

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.330
you can do to improve the
audio that you record,

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.430
and the really fundamental
things that you

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.360
need to be thinking about.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.600
So some of you again, as
with the camera, I mean,

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.440
if some of you have audio
recording experience

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.670
or are very interested in audio,
this may seem like old hat

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:10.840
or very simple to you.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.900
But we're going to be
starting at a basic level that

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.300
assumes no prior knowledge.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:20.020
Before we do that though,
I want to, as we discussed,

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.510
go back and take a look
at some of the stuff that

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:24.790
was shot last week.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.020
Some very interesting
choices were made,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.320
and we're going to go
through it and talk

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.950
about what worked,
what could be improved,

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.520
some of the technical
challenges that were faced.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.110
And then based on
that, we can do

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:41.890
a little bit of a review and
an expansion of various things

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:46.720
you can do with the camera, both
in terms of framing and film

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.670
grammar, as we've
been talking about,

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.450
as well as technical things.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.600
So I'm going to dive right in.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:01.350
Put together just a
few clips, all silent.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:05.880
I took the audio out because
it was all a camera mic and--

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:12.890
Yep.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:13.972
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:13.972 --> 00:02:15.430
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well,
let's find out.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:22.280
OK, starting right
with a close up.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.670
So if you recall what
the exercise was,

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.470
it was film seven or eight
different kinds of things,

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:30.580
including portraits of people.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:31.990
And this is--

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:33.590
I picked a random sampling.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:35.350
It's not everything
that everyone shot,

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:39.340
but it's a series of shots that
were interesting for one reason

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:44.000
or that had challenges
for one reason or another.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.720
So this is the first thing.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.830
And this is just, first of
all, in terms of framing,

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.870
a really nice portrait.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.400
It's a really nice shot.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.130
I mean, it's very engaging,
it's very interesting,

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.960
the framing is nice.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.910
It's a little bit
off center so there's

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.280
some energy in the frame.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.950
The challenge, of course, is--

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:09.460
and I should go back--

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:14.740
there are some technical issues,
the primary one being exposure.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:18.940
And you can see that we're
losing a ton of detail

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.060
in the brighter
parts of the frame.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.800
And you were probably a couple
of stops overexposed here.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.190
Now what you can do,
of course, in editing

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:33.080
is attempt to fix that,
bring it down, adjust,

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:34.570
and we can make it look better.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:39.340
But this is fairly extreme
on the overexposure side.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:44.890
And it would be, obviously,
a lot better to get it right.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.320
Now what's interesting
is that this is also

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.240
kind of an example of
breaking the rules.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:54.490
I mean, this is a very kind
of dreamy, soft feel to it,

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:58.210
and fashion photography
often has that kind of

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:01.090
blown out whites that
you're looking at here.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.580
But I don't think
this was intentional.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:06.680
I'm not sure, maybe it was.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.440
It sort of works.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.460
But I would say, concentrate on
getting a good exposure first.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:16.300
And if you're not
sure of your own skill

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:20.980
at reading, judging exposure
in manual, what you can do

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.655
is go to auto exposure and
zoom in on the person's face.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:31.030
Find out what auto exposure
wants to do, go back to manual,

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:35.690
and then the face is probably
going to be relatively properly

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:36.190
exposed.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:38.830
It depends on skin
tone of course.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:44.140
Most cameras are designed
for Caucasian skin tone.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:47.560
And so if you are filming
people with darker skin tones,

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.390
you may want to open up a bit.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:50.180
Dave?

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:51.790
DAVE STRIPINUS: You can
also use the spot meter.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:52.150
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yes.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:53.775
DAVE STRIPINUS: It's
on the second page

00:04:53.775 --> 00:04:54.730
of the personal menu.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:57.790
I think it's the bottom
right, or something like that.

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:02.530
But the spot meter, it acts
a lot like the spot focus.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:07.720
Once you select it, you use your
finger right on the LCD screen,

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:11.680
and you just press what it
is want to properly expose,

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.280
and it will expose that.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.510
And something like this
background would be blown out,

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.800
but it's not really what
you care about anyway.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:19.798
It's all white anyway.

00:05:19.798 --> 00:05:21.340
CHRIS BOEBEL: In
most cases, you want

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.360
to expose for people's
faces properly, which

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.110
is why you can either
zoom in on the face,

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:30.760
get an exposure in auto, or use
the spot meter, pull back out,

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.140
and get a decent exposure.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.608
Now, if you're shooting a very,
very wide shot, of course,

00:05:35.608 --> 00:05:36.650
that may not be the case.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:40.390
It may be fine to
underexpose a person's face

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:45.010
in order to get the
environment properly exposed.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:49.270
And again, it's just the
way these cameras are set up

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:53.150
that they are set up to expose,
as I said, Caucasian skin tone.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:55.150
So for darker skin
tones, you may

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.530
want to open up a 1/2 a
stop, a stop, whatever.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:59.113
And that's something
that you're going

00:05:59.113 --> 00:06:00.820
to have to learn to judge.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:05.380
But as I said, I mean this is
kind of a very pleasing shot.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:06.610
The framing is very nice.

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:12.750
A little bit shaky, a
little bit uncertain.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.320
Again, kind of interesting.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:23.342
Just a very interesting way
of shooting a person I mean,

00:06:23.342 --> 00:06:24.300
there's just something.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:25.630
I like these two shots.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.040
Again, there's an
exposure issue.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:29.890
I think there's also a bit of
a white balance issue with both

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:33.370
of these, but it's
a little bit hard

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.430
to tell because the exposure is
so high that we're also losing

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:39.010
a lot of detail in the color.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:44.260
So again, think
about those four F's.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:46.780
White balance, of course,
not being one of the F's,

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:49.510
but the point is that
you need to think

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:52.510
about your technical aspects.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:54.230
And it just becomes
kind of routine.

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:56.770
Set up the camera, make
sure it's in focus,

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:58.870
make sure it's
properly exposed, think

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:02.920
about what your focal
length is in relation

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:04.930
to this kind of shot
you want to shoot,

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:07.510
make sure that the color
balance, the white balance

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:08.260
is correct.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:09.760
And you just kind
of run through it,

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:12.770
and then you're ready to go.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:15.850
But again, both of these
shots are very nice portraits.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:23.010
So here's another one.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:25.740
This is an interesting frame.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.720
I like the off-center
nature of the frame.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:32.890
The big issue here is focus.

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:37.530
And as you can see, her
face is out of focus.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.427
The background is
sharper than the face.

00:07:39.427 --> 00:07:41.010
And one of the things
that we're going

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:47.190
to review at the end of this is
ways of getting proper focus.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:49.440
I think Dave and I kind
of ran through focus

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.590
relatively quickly,
and it's worth

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:54.690
reviewing and re-emphasizing.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.520
Because focus, again,
is one of those kind

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.407
of non-negotiable items.

00:07:59.407 --> 00:08:00.990
There are lots of
rules you can break.

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:04.913
You can overexpose the frame
if you know what you're doing

00:08:04.913 --> 00:08:06.330
and you have a
really good reason.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.490
You can frame things
unconventionally.

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:12.090
But focus, it should
always be in focus.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:15.960
Even if you blur it later, you
really need to shoot in focus.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.310
So that's just kind of a basic.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:23.192
And we will talk about ways of
making sure that what you want

00:08:23.192 --> 00:08:24.900
is in focus, and not
what you don't want.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:28.560
I mean again here, the issue
is that the background is

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.700
sharper than the person's face.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:38.970
And I think here, I think you
may have been working on that.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:40.330
Didn't quite come off.

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:41.799
But anyway, we'll
talk about focus

00:08:41.799 --> 00:08:48.000
and again, how it just kind
of becomes second nature,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.450
that habit to check focus
on whatever it is that you

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:54.600
want to have emphasized.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:59.540
We have shambling
giant cruising through.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.270
And both of us are so
awkward on camera, Dave,

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:03.410
you'll see me in a minute.

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:04.495
It's horrible.

00:09:04.495 --> 00:09:05.870
DAVE STRIPINUS:
Look like there's

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:09.170
a bridge you're
going to jump off.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:11.810
Now what's interesting is that
you shot this several times,

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:16.700
and that was great.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:18.340
The first time,
you see, again, we

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:20.840
talked about where people should
be positioned in the frame.

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:24.020
The camera really wasn't
keeping up with Dave.

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:26.840
He was just really riding
the right side of the frame.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:29.540
So you were clearly
working on that.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.742
And this one is somewhat better.

00:09:31.742 --> 00:09:33.950
CHRIS BOEBEL: It would still
be a more effective shot

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:35.992
if he could always stay
a little bit on the left,

00:09:35.992 --> 00:09:37.970
since he's walking to
the right, because it's

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:40.418
just better to show where
the person's heading,

00:09:40.418 --> 00:09:42.710
and it gives them more energy,
like that they're moving

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:44.750
into the space on the right.

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:47.493
But it also can be tricky.

00:09:47.493 --> 00:09:49.160
Dave, you're not
moving very fast there.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:51.863
But if people are moving fast,
obviously it can be tricky.

00:09:51.863 --> 00:09:53.780
DAVE STRIPINUS: It's
called pushing the frame,

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:57.650
like when it looks like you're
pushing the frame as they walk,

00:09:57.650 --> 00:09:58.740
rather than the camera.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:00.200
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:01.970
It's like the unresponsive
camera person,

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:05.450
like oh, I didn't expect that.

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:11.060
The other thing, just
to review is headroom.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:22.950
You can see that it would
be again preferable given

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:25.260
the size of the shot and so on.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.480
If you weren't chopping
the top of his head.

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:30.900
Although it starts out
framed pretty well.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.790
Eyes are up in the top 1/3 here.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:36.810
The zoom out is a little
shaky, but it's a good idea.

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:38.220
This one's a little
better again.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.140
Also, I love the
reveal of this guy.

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:45.370
What?

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:47.770
Anyway, that was
unintentional, but that there's

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:49.780
a good example of that.

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:52.240
It's a good example of
revealing something.

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:58.000
I mean, this is a classic film
trope that one piece of action

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.710
leads into something else
that then reveals itself.

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:04.090
So if in fact Dave was
just an extra in your film

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:06.160
and that guy was
your main character,

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:09.298
and he was freaking out because
he was late for something

00:11:09.298 --> 00:11:10.840
and he didn't know
where he was, that

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:14.380
would be kind of an interesting
way to introduce that.

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:17.140
Unintentional, but
again, think about how

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:19.720
you reveal things using
one kind of action

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:22.090
to reveal another or
motion to reveal something.

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:25.030
You don't always have to have
everything square, presented

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:25.930
boom in the frame.

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:34.580
So I think this is kind
of an interesting angle.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:43.430
I mean, we talked about Varta,
his use of very low, very high,

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:46.050
very different kinds of angles.

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:49.910
This is a not quite as
intense version of that.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:51.890
But again, it's not head on.

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:54.020
It actually works
really well because you

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:58.220
have these diagonals
going up in the staircase.

00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:01.250
And it really emphasizes
what the action

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:03.510
is, this person going
on the ladder, and then

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.090
down the stairs.

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:07.170
Now one small thing--

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:09.770
I think I mentioned this
in the first class--

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:11.510
about being decisive
with the camera.

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:14.130
Act like what you're doing even
if you feel that you don't.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:15.922
And one of the things
that's going on there

00:12:15.922 --> 00:12:18.500
is so you see those little
zoom in, the zoom out,

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:19.650
they're very tentative.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:21.290
Like, should I be tighter?

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:22.340
No, maybe wider?

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:23.510
No, I'm not sure.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:27.580
And you just have to
act like what you want,

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:28.950
even if you aren't sure.

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:33.042
So if you're thinking,
I want to zoom in here,

00:12:33.042 --> 00:12:34.250
you want to start zooming in.

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:35.630
And then until
you find something

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:41.150
that seems worth zooming in
to, and then pull back out.

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:42.590
Zooms are, as
we've talked about,

00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:44.000
zooms are kind of tricky.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:48.440
They're kind of too easy,
and people a lot of people

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:51.080
are just completely
against zooms

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:53.740
when it comes to
documentary work and so on,

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:55.490
just because it's easy
to sit in one place

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:57.560
and just get a bunch
of different shots.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:59.893
But if you're going to use
that, if you're going to move

00:12:59.893 --> 00:13:03.350
the camera in any way, zooming
or panning or hand-held,

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:06.230
you really want to, as I
said, kind of act like what

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:08.870
you're doing, even if you
feel you really don't.

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:12.350
So if I'm shooting this class.

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:14.420
I move over to Dave.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:16.520
Oh, Dave's not doing
anything interesting.

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:18.770
Well I still did that,
so I'm going to do it.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:20.930
I'm going to sell that shot.

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:24.590
And if it's a terrible shot,
if he's picking his nose

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:26.570
or something, I'll
bail out of it

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:29.400
as quickly as I possibly can,
and get back to something else.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:31.010
So I won't don't
waste a lot of time.

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:36.230
What I don't do is,
is that interesting?

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:37.230
Well maybe.

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:38.480
No, oh, he's picking his nose.

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:41.000
Because then I
didn't get anything.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:44.390
So again, it's just something
that it kind of becomes

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:46.010
second nature.

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:48.800
And did e talk about David
McDougall and the film trance?

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:51.230
I forget.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:53.050
Do you want to say
something about that?

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:54.890
AUDIENCE: No, I'm not
sure what you have to say.

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:56.348
CHRIS BOEBEL: Oh,
well just there's

00:13:56.348 --> 00:13:59.090
a documentary filmmaker,
David McDougall.

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:02.450
And I don't remember if he's
on the reading list this year.

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:04.910
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:06.710
CHRIS BOEBEL: What's that?

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:07.880
Oh, John Roush?

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:09.320
Oh, film trance.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:12.260
Oh, OK, I thought that
was-- no, McDougall talks, I

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:13.860
guess he uses a different term.

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:17.690
So anyway, he talks
about it, too.

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:22.280
But so John Roush, a
filmmaker that we'll

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.260
be looking at
later in the class,

00:14:24.260 --> 00:14:28.340
talks about something that
he calls a cinetrance, which

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:32.870
is basically the mode you get
in when you're shooting a scene.

00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:35.360
And a documentary scene, a
scene that you're not really

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:39.320
controlling, where
you're so immersed in it.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:42.185
And people are kind of
interacting with you,

00:14:42.185 --> 00:14:43.580
you're interacting with them.

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:46.520
You're making choices
as the action unfolds

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:50.810
about what you're shooting,
how you choose to shoot it,

00:14:50.810 --> 00:14:52.490
the angle you're
choosing to shoot,

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:53.907
the next thing
you're going to do.

00:14:53.907 --> 00:14:56.840
And the choices that you make
almost instinctively when

00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:59.060
you're that immersed in a scene.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:05.370
And another way to talk about
it is that whatever you're doing

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:05.870
is right.

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:07.155
It's very spontaneous.

00:15:07.155 --> 00:15:09.530
Again, when I say, act like
what you're doing even if you

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:12.680
don't, it's kind of similar.

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:16.460
Because you're
responding to whatever's

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:17.640
happening around you.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:20.498
And so you can't
make a wrong choice.

00:15:20.498 --> 00:15:22.790
If you do make a wrong choice,
you get out of it really

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:23.570
quickly.

00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:25.580
But the choice you've
made instinctively,

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:29.870
if you trust yourself, trust
yourself to follow that up.

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:33.490
Why did I want to
pan over to Dave?

00:15:33.490 --> 00:15:34.186
Maybe--

00:15:34.186 --> 00:15:36.970
AUDIENCE: Just following
up on that film trance,

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:39.430
there is something very
hypnotic about it, which

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:42.260
was part of the discussion.

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:46.220
How do you get so-- and
I have that machine--

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:48.290
but when you do do it and
you get really into it,

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:50.270
it does become kind of like
hypnotic and basically,

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:51.645
they just talk
about, say, people

00:15:51.645 --> 00:15:55.682
who do war photography
or war filmmaking,

00:15:55.682 --> 00:15:57.890
that they had a sense of
themselves being invincible,

00:15:57.890 --> 00:16:00.155
because you get so
caught up in the frame

00:16:00.155 --> 00:16:02.030
that you just follow
everything, and it's not

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:03.780
like a normal
experience of the world.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:05.420
And if you can become
really addictive

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:09.170
to see through the camera frame.

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:10.820
And if you remember
from Varta there's

00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:15.260
a discussion with Hal Kaufman,
who was the brother of Varta,

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:16.510
who did a lot of the shooting.

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:20.170
And he went on this vacation,
and he didn't bring his camera.

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:22.600
And he was so disturbed seeing
the world without a camera,

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:24.940
because he'd become so
addicted to seeing the world

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:28.350
to the camera, that
the [INAUDIBLE] him.

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:30.100
So there is just
something really hypnotic

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:33.280
about once you're seeing the
world through this camera eye,

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:34.798
just get kind of caught.

00:16:34.798 --> 00:16:36.590
CHRIS BOEBEL: I had
that experience I mean,

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:38.770
I've never done anything
like war photography,

00:16:38.770 --> 00:16:42.010
but I was shooting aerobatics
in a light plane at one point.

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:44.835
And we were doing all
these insane, crazy things

00:16:44.835 --> 00:16:46.960
that I would have been
absolutely terrified to have

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:47.750
been involved in.

00:16:47.750 --> 00:16:50.660
But I was shooting
it, and I didn't care.

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:53.283
I was just like,
whoa, this is amazing.

00:16:53.283 --> 00:16:55.450
We're diving and then we're
flying along the highway

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:57.400
a foot off the ground, and
then we're climbing vertically

00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:58.080
and rolling.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:16:59.630
And it was just great.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:01.010
I loved it.

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:02.440
But if I had not
had the camera, I

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:05.290
would have been completely
terrified, because that's not

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:06.250
my idea of fun.

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:09.430
So anyway.

00:17:09.430 --> 00:17:11.869
So let's moving on.

00:17:11.869 --> 00:17:16.000
So this is, we
did the following.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:17.819
So this is quite a cool shot.

00:17:17.819 --> 00:17:21.240
The way it's introduced with
the shadow and then pans up.

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:25.810
Doors locked, of course.

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:28.540
So we tried that several times.

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:31.240
Just full disclosure, I think
the first couple were not

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:32.000
as successful.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:33.773
So that's another
thing to think about.

00:17:33.773 --> 00:17:35.440
If you're interacting
with their subject

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:38.110
and your subject is
cooperative, and it

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:40.570
doesn't work the first few
times, you can keep doing it.

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:43.510
You saw that again
with Dave and the pan.

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:48.370
So it took a few times for
you to work out how would

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:49.900
the shadow reveal the person?

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:50.860
Oh, that didn't work.

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:52.930
Well try something else.

00:17:52.930 --> 00:17:56.590
But that was a nice shot.

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:57.670
Another.

00:17:57.670 --> 00:17:59.455
Of course, you can't
resist a mirror.

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:08.350
So here we have some
hand-held following.

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:14.580
This is a little shaky,
but quite a nice shot.

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:18.580
And just to talk about
handheld for a moment,

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:20.550
one of the things that
I think we'll do--

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:22.710
I talk about how we're
going to go over focus--

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:25.680
I think we should also talk
about techniques for handheld,

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:29.070
holding the camera,
how to smooth yourself.

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:31.090
So there's that
technical aspect of it,

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:33.690
but then there's also
the choice of how do you

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:36.600
frame your subjects and how
do you choose whether or not

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:37.770
you're going handheld.

00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:40.800
And what works about this
is that you're following

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:44.120
a person, first of all.

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:46.830
A handheld shot that could
have better be shot on a tripod

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:49.560
is just a shaky shot, or
potentially shaky shot.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:52.880
This works because
you're following

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:54.210
a person who's walking.

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:57.840
I mean, it's obvious,
but it's worth saying.

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:02.745
Also it's a wide shot,
and wide focal lengths

00:19:02.745 --> 00:19:04.995
work much better in handheld
because they're smoother.

00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:09.880
And it's also kind
of a pleasing frame.

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:13.405
You have these
horizontals converging

00:19:13.405 --> 00:19:14.530
in the center of the frame.

00:19:17.295 --> 00:19:18.670
So here we have
another handheld,

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:20.860
another in our genre of
tripod carrying shots.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:30.000
This is kind of a little washed
out because of the light.

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:39.430
So again, kind of shaky,
but I mean, going downstairs

00:19:39.430 --> 00:19:46.045
is very difficult.

00:19:46.045 --> 00:19:47.420
One of the things
that might help

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:51.320
would be paying more
attention to the verticals

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:53.330
and horizontals.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:57.590
You see, we're at a
real tilted angle here.

00:19:57.590 --> 00:19:59.220
What's interesting
about this shot

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:02.023
is that the frame changes a lot.

00:20:02.023 --> 00:20:03.440
And this is another
thing that you

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:05.510
can do with a moving
camera that's kind of cool.

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:08.340
So it starts out with kind
of a wide shot of me walking,

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:10.340
and then it turns into
an over-the-shoulder shot

00:20:10.340 --> 00:20:11.215
of me walking.

00:20:11.215 --> 00:20:12.590
And then the camera
comes around,

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:16.760
and now I'm in a closer shot,
and you're walking backwards.

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:21.620
So one of the reasons to do a
handheld shot or a moving shot

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:25.310
is so that you can make
the frame more fluid.

00:20:25.310 --> 00:20:27.620
You can do a wide shot that
turns into a close-up that

00:20:27.620 --> 00:20:29.328
turns into an
over-the-shoulder shot that

00:20:29.328 --> 00:20:32.120
turns into a medium shot.

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:35.900
And again, what works about
this is that, and the fact

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:38.540
that I'm being followed.

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:41.630
That there's action
in the frame that's

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.210
initiating the moving camera.

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:48.060
And my main suggestion
would be first of all,

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:50.790
as I said, to really think about
the verticals and horizontals,

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:55.040
and try to avoid that
seasickness feeling.

00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:57.270
There's also an issue
with exposure here.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:59.270
And this is
something that you'll

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:01.970
have to make a choice about if
you try to do shots like this.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:04.040
I'm going through all sorts
of different light sources,

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:05.960
and all sorts of different
levels of exposure.

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:07.760
It's very dark, then
it's very bright,

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:09.180
then it's very dark again.

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:11.580
And what we did was
we picked one exposure

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:13.130
and we stayed on that.

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:16.820
So you can see that
sometimes I'm underexposed,

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:19.550
sometimes I'm overexposed,
the windows are blown out.

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:22.110
And the issue for
you to think about--

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:24.620
and there are actually
reasons to do both--

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:26.180
is do you want to
do it that way,

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:27.983
do you want to leave
the camera on auto--

00:21:27.983 --> 00:21:30.650
the problem with that being that
the camera will start breathing

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:33.800
and changing sometimes
when you don't want it to.

00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:38.250
Or do you want to manually
change your exposure as you go?

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:43.790
So you could start out
at a much higher f-stop,

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:46.960
and then you could stop down
as the bright light hits

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:47.460
the camera.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:49.430
And then when you come
around the corner here,

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:52.472
you could open the iris again.

00:21:52.472 --> 00:21:54.680
And you would have to do
that manually while you were

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:57.860
walking, which is a trick
to learn, especially

00:21:57.860 --> 00:22:00.470
with these little cameras
and the little iris.

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:02.850
But it's a choice to make.

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:04.847
Which do you want to do?

00:22:04.847 --> 00:22:07.430
It's going to really depend on
your preference as a filmmaker,

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:10.550
and exactly what kind of
shot you're going for.

00:22:17.325 --> 00:22:18.950
So here we have
another following shot.

00:22:18.950 --> 00:22:22.580
There's a bit of a white
balance issue here.

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:24.590
So we're a bit orange.

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:26.345
I think we've come
in from outside.

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:34.387
So what I like about
that shot is the fact

00:22:34.387 --> 00:22:35.970
that you start out
in a very wide shot

00:22:35.970 --> 00:22:37.140
and come into a close-up.

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.660
And that's really powerful.

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:42.570
That's a very simple way
of introducing a character.

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:45.420
I see that sign, I go, hey,
you're the main character.

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:46.410
I'm following this guy.

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:47.820
This is great.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:51.310
So that's really powerful.

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:54.000
There are also some
kind of neat frames.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.340
And the frame is
changing in a nice way.

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:59.490
I mean, you're showing off
the architecture of Stata

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:01.518
and that staircase,
which is quite striking.

00:23:01.518 --> 00:23:03.060
And then as I said,
it's starting out

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:05.970
in a very wide shot and
coming into a close-up.

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:09.098
White balance again,
you change your shot,

00:23:09.098 --> 00:23:10.890
you should always check
your white balance.

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:13.213
I mean, unless it's
really the same lighting,

00:23:13.213 --> 00:23:14.130
you want to change it.

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:16.822
Certainly if you come in to the
interior from being outside,

00:23:16.822 --> 00:23:18.030
you need to re-white balance.

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:19.890
Even within an
environment like Stata,

00:23:19.890 --> 00:23:22.440
where you've got
overheads and big windows

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:25.320
and all sorts of mixed lighting
situations, if you change,

00:23:25.320 --> 00:23:26.820
you want to check
the white balance.

00:23:26.820 --> 00:23:28.920
Because parts of
Stata are almost

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:30.480
like daylight,
and parts of Stata

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:32.370
are a very different
color temperature,

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:34.650
because they have fluorescents.

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:36.480
They might have halogens.

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:39.190
I mean, there's all sorts of
crazy stuff going on in there.

00:23:39.190 --> 00:23:42.090
So you want to change
and check every time.

00:23:45.750 --> 00:23:48.510
So here we have
filming an activity.

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:51.200
So this is a close-up,
or what we call sometimes

00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:52.160
an insert shot.

00:23:56.530 --> 00:23:57.130
Nice shot.

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:04.130
So the only thing there is
you had a really nice shot,

00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:05.630
and you just hold it.

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:07.940
Don't worry about changing it.

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:09.142
It was working really well.

00:24:09.142 --> 00:24:10.850
And then suddenly
there was that decision

00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:12.470
to like, oh, wait, why go wide.

00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:14.390
Oh, wait, I'm going
to not go wide.

00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:16.580
And again, everyone does that.

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:20.120
I mean, even very experienced
camera people do that.

00:24:20.120 --> 00:24:23.240
But making a choice
and sticking with it

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:26.990
is some of the best
advice I can give.

00:24:26.990 --> 00:24:30.890
So if the choice there was to
go wide, go all the way wide.

00:24:30.890 --> 00:24:33.350
Show the whole scene.

00:24:33.350 --> 00:24:37.520
And then after you've done
that for a few seconds,

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:39.690
if that seems to have been
maybe the wrong choice,

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:40.580
you can go back in.

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:44.510
But going back and forth,
censoring yourself, going,

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:45.260
I want to be wide.

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:46.550
Oh no, I want to be close.

00:24:46.550 --> 00:24:47.598
And it's not just zoom.

00:24:47.598 --> 00:24:49.640
I mean, I've shown a couple
of examples of zooms.

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:52.910
But again, I want to show Chris.

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:54.470
No, I want show Dave.

00:24:54.470 --> 00:24:57.260
No, I want to show Sabrina.

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:59.255
That is how you end
up with nothing.

00:25:03.550 --> 00:25:05.190
So here we have another zoom.

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:08.670
And again, I'm not
a huge fan of zooms.

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:11.670
But that one kind of
works because it's

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:12.720
revealing something.

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:14.290
Again, there's an
exposure issue.

00:25:14.290 --> 00:25:15.840
It's overexposed.

00:25:20.430 --> 00:25:21.950
But you have this
group of people,

00:25:21.950 --> 00:25:24.675
and you're revealing the
sculpture and the environment.

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:34.520
Now this is a very pretty
shot which again, is

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:40.110
marred by that indecisiveness.

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:43.850
So I could imagine
that same shot

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:47.480
being this very beautiful
environmental shot, just

00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:50.930
with a more decisive
tilt up and tilt down.

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:55.310
So you could be doing a
very nice, smooth tilt

00:25:55.310 --> 00:25:56.370
up to the sky.

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:58.670
You could go all
the way to the sky.

00:25:58.670 --> 00:26:01.220
Or you could come
down from the sky,

00:26:01.220 --> 00:26:05.180
reverse it, and find
the people walking.

00:26:05.180 --> 00:26:08.210
But whatever you do,
you want to be decisive

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:14.810
so that you don't have that
uncertainty in the camera.

00:26:14.810 --> 00:26:16.100
See what I'm talking about.

00:26:16.100 --> 00:26:16.910
Should I start?

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:18.132
OK, I think, I will.

00:26:18.132 --> 00:26:18.840
Where am I going?

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:19.430
I'm not sure.

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:25.560
But again, very
pretty idea of a shot.

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:34.077
And here we have a really
great shot, nice self-portrait.

00:26:34.077 --> 00:26:35.660
The only thing I
would say about that,

00:26:35.660 --> 00:26:39.800
again, there's a little bit
of indecisiveness at the end.

00:26:39.800 --> 00:26:42.140
And it's the kind of
shot that would probably

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:44.000
be better on a tripod.

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:46.760
I mean, there may have
been a time issue.

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:48.230
Like oh, well you
see this thing,

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:50.355
and you don't want to set
up the tripod, and so on.

00:26:50.355 --> 00:26:51.930
And sometimes that's
understandable.

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:54.200
But if you have time, you
see a great shot like that,

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:56.840
if you set up the tripod,
you'll have the same shot

00:26:56.840 --> 00:26:59.510
and it'll just be nice
and much smoother.

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:04.490
Because the energy or
shakiness of the handheld frame

00:27:04.490 --> 00:27:07.100
there doesn't really
add to the shot.

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:07.950
But it's great.

00:27:07.950 --> 00:27:09.920
It's a very pretty
idea for a shot,

00:27:09.920 --> 00:27:14.105
and it and it's a nice
review of you guys working.

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:18.280
So here we have,
I'm going to end

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:20.710
with a couple of
voyeuristic shots.

00:27:20.710 --> 00:27:22.480
This one, I mean there's
an exposure issue

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:24.710
and a white balance issue.

00:27:24.710 --> 00:27:31.600
But I included it because it
feels a little bit voyeuristic.

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:33.970
And the reason for
that is that it's

00:27:33.970 --> 00:27:35.500
shot on a long lens,
so everything's

00:27:35.500 --> 00:27:36.292
kind of compressed.

00:27:36.292 --> 00:27:39.820
It feels like the camera is
far away from the action.

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:45.670
So imagine shooting that same
two dogs at play on a wide lens

00:27:45.670 --> 00:27:48.850
where you're right
up in the action.

00:27:48.850 --> 00:27:51.640
It would feel really,
really different.

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:53.380
Whether you were
handheld or wide,

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:56.080
it would feel much more
intimate, much more

00:27:56.080 --> 00:28:00.670
like the camera people were
connected to the action.

00:28:00.670 --> 00:28:03.670
More like, you knew these
dogs, you knew the owners,

00:28:03.670 --> 00:28:07.450
you were part of the activity.

00:28:07.450 --> 00:28:10.300
That is, I mean it's
a cool shot, aside

00:28:10.300 --> 00:28:13.570
from the technical
issues, and sometimes you

00:28:13.570 --> 00:28:15.670
may want to do
this intentionally.

00:28:15.670 --> 00:28:17.620
It pushes us away
from the action.

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:21.490
It feels like we're kind
of viewing it from afar,

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:22.780
because literally, we are.

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:27.380
And speaking of voyeurism.

00:28:32.990 --> 00:28:35.490
So I included that again
because first of all,

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:38.810
it's a very interesting
example of a rack focus,

00:28:38.810 --> 00:28:40.310
like revealing
something with focus.

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:42.227
And secondly, because
it feels so voyeuristic,

00:28:42.227 --> 00:28:43.790
obviously because
of the activity.

00:28:43.790 --> 00:28:45.457
But also because of
the way it's framed.

00:28:45.457 --> 00:28:49.410
It's a long lens, we focus into
it, you see this little moment,

00:28:49.410 --> 00:28:51.240
and then we fade out.

00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:54.800
So it's actually it's
very successful as a shot,

00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:59.600
but it's a very specific way,
a very specific emotional way

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:00.860
of looking at that scene.

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:02.960
I mean, again, imagine
if you were right down

00:29:02.960 --> 00:29:05.540
there with the people,
filming them kissing.

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:08.930
That would be really
different, because would there

00:29:08.930 --> 00:29:12.230
be an implicit consent
that you were there,

00:29:12.230 --> 00:29:15.530
they were there, that there
was much more intimacy.

00:29:15.530 --> 00:29:20.960
This is, for better or worse
very, very, very distant.

00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:21.950
And it's cool.

00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:23.448
I mean, it works.

00:29:23.448 --> 00:29:24.740
I'm not saying it doesn't work.

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:27.560
I'm saying it gives you a
very specific emotional read

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:33.080
of the situation, and the
camera's relationship.

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:34.760
Is that kiss more
elicit than the kiss

00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:36.755
that you would
have filmed closer?

00:29:36.755 --> 00:29:37.880
It feels a lot more elicit.

00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:43.800
And then the last shot
here, the Kino eye,

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:47.100
think this is a very cool shot.

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:48.855
This was our OpenCourseWare
camera person.

00:29:53.882 --> 00:29:55.590
So I thought that was
a very pretty shot,

00:29:55.590 --> 00:29:57.580
and a nice way to end it.

00:29:57.580 --> 00:30:00.100
So anyway, any thoughts or
questions about any of this

00:30:00.100 --> 00:30:00.600
stuff?

00:30:06.230 --> 00:30:07.885
Ideas?

00:30:07.885 --> 00:30:08.385
Concerns?

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:16.140
OK, so what I thought
we would do next,

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:18.320
and it's going to take a
minute or two to set up,

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:22.310
is just talk a little bit
about some of the things

00:30:22.310 --> 00:30:26.000
that came up in this stuff.

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:28.940
We're going to try to hook
up a camera to the projector.

00:30:28.940 --> 00:30:30.530
I can show you a
couple of things

00:30:30.530 --> 00:30:32.570
that you can be thinking
about when you shoot.

00:30:32.570 --> 00:30:35.240
And then we're going to talk
a bit more about film grammar

00:30:35.240 --> 00:30:37.190
before we switch over to audio.

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:43.760
So let's see, I guess
I should unplug this.

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:08.340
Yeah, I mean this won't
take too long, but--

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:26.970
Here we go.

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:32.260
OK, so let's just start talking
about setting up a shot here.

00:31:32.260 --> 00:31:35.910
I mean, this is not a
very appealing shot.

00:31:35.910 --> 00:31:37.050
The white balance is wrong.

00:31:37.050 --> 00:31:38.467
DAVE STRIPINUS:
It's a good chance

00:31:38.467 --> 00:31:40.020
to talk about
angles, if you shoot

00:31:40.020 --> 00:31:41.250
from below rather than above.

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:43.290
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well yeah,
but let's first talk

00:31:43.290 --> 00:31:45.150
about white balance
and exposure.

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:48.360
Because this is not happening.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:51.000
So how would I fix this?

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:52.570
First thing I want
to do, of course,

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:55.775
is make sure that my
white balance is correct.

00:31:55.775 --> 00:31:57.150
If I don't have
a piece of paper,

00:31:57.150 --> 00:32:00.310
I can grab something else.

00:32:00.310 --> 00:32:05.510
So that is probably
close enough for now.

00:32:05.510 --> 00:32:09.450
Second thing I might want to
do is get a rough exposure.

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:14.720
So I'm exposing up.

00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:17.780
Now what exactly my exposure is
here might depend on my frame,

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:20.100
and I'll show you what I mean.

00:32:20.100 --> 00:32:22.805
If I zoom in on
Dave, I get my focus.

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:34.020
And I may want to expose
more if I'm really going

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:35.653
to be in close up on Dave.

00:32:35.653 --> 00:32:37.320
DAVE STRIPINUS: I
also have an odd face.

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:40.440
They get those shadows under
the eyes because of my eye.

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:42.540
CHRIS BOEBEL: Oh,
stop making excuses.

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:49.200
All right, so this would be
like a close-up, potentially.

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:50.950
It's a really tough
lighting situation.

00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:54.060
I might ask Dave to move
if I felt like I could,

00:32:54.060 --> 00:32:56.950
depending on the environment.

00:32:56.950 --> 00:32:59.670
Yeah, I'm afraid too, though.

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:02.340
So if I go out
wide again, see now

00:33:02.340 --> 00:33:04.150
it looks really overexposed.

00:33:04.150 --> 00:33:09.780
So again, I'm going to adjust
my exposure a little bit.

00:33:09.780 --> 00:33:13.210
What's really killing me is
the backlight of the window.

00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:16.710
So what I could say
is, excuse me, sir?

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:19.770
Could you take a step just
a little forward please?

00:33:19.770 --> 00:33:20.430
Thank you.

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:31.503
And now

00:33:31.503 --> 00:33:32.920
DAVE STRIPINUS:
Chris, you're in--

00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:33.920
CHRIS BOEBEL: I'm sorry.

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:48.310
So now it's a little bit easier
to get a proper exposure.

00:33:48.310 --> 00:33:51.640
So the trick with
focus, which I don't

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:53.950
think we emphasized
enough last week,

00:33:53.950 --> 00:33:56.320
is that if you're in a
tough focus situation,

00:33:56.320 --> 00:33:59.440
or pretty much in any
situation, what you want to do

00:33:59.440 --> 00:34:02.755
is zoom in and get focus.

00:34:07.130 --> 00:34:09.790
And in the case of shooting
people, what you want

00:34:09.790 --> 00:34:12.590
to be in focus are the eyes.

00:34:12.590 --> 00:34:14.590
And then you can zoom
back out to whatever frame

00:34:14.590 --> 00:34:19.010
it is that you wanted to have.

00:34:19.010 --> 00:34:24.100
So even if I'm way out here--

00:34:24.100 --> 00:34:26.530
and here I might want
to go down in terms

00:34:26.530 --> 00:34:29.020
of my exposure a little bit--

00:34:29.020 --> 00:34:31.494
his eyes are what I cared
about being in focus.

00:34:38.070 --> 00:34:40.123
And again, if I
come over here, I

00:34:40.123 --> 00:34:41.790
might want to rechange
my white balance.

00:34:41.790 --> 00:34:43.415
Because this is a
very mixed situation.

00:34:43.415 --> 00:34:46.830
Dave is much more in
daylight over here

00:34:46.830 --> 00:34:50.250
than he would be over here.

00:34:50.250 --> 00:34:55.350
So I might want to change
my white balance again.

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:57.210
And remember again,
the white balance

00:34:57.210 --> 00:35:01.197
is set to the assign button
on the right of the camera.

00:35:01.197 --> 00:35:03.030
So I'm going to zoom
in on a piece of paper.

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:08.360
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:11.080
CHRIS BOEBEL:
Well, I mean, yeah,

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:12.714
bring white paper with you.

00:35:12.714 --> 00:35:14.214
DAVE STRIPINUS:
Looks a little blue.

00:35:14.214 --> 00:35:16.790
CHRIS BOEBEL: That really
does look blue, doesn't it?

00:35:16.790 --> 00:35:19.510
Doesn't seem like a
very good white balance.

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:22.520
DAVE STRIPINUS: Do you want
me to hold a piece of paper?

00:35:22.520 --> 00:35:24.020
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, right.

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:27.080
The table was not a really
good choice, actually.

00:35:27.080 --> 00:35:35.595
But this seems-- anyway.

00:35:38.706 --> 00:35:43.690
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:43.690 --> 00:35:46.510
CHRIS BOEBEL: Then everything
will be off by that percent.

00:35:46.510 --> 00:35:49.360
So yeah, what Dennis
mentioned was that this,

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:53.440
I set it to a table here,
which isn't really white.

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:56.350
It's got a little bit
of yellowish in it.

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:02.313
So I'm fooling the
camera a little bit.

00:36:02.313 --> 00:36:03.730
And you can do
that intentionally,

00:36:03.730 --> 00:36:06.010
but it's probably better
to do that kind of thing

00:36:06.010 --> 00:36:07.008
in post-production.

00:36:07.008 --> 00:36:08.800
DAVE STRIPINUS: If you
get an approximation

00:36:08.800 --> 00:36:11.200
it's pretty easy to fix
white balance in post.

00:36:13.608 --> 00:36:15.900
CHRIS BOEBEL: And yeah, some
of the things that we just

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:19.290
looked at that were off
probably are somewhat fixable.

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:23.330
So that looks better.

00:36:26.960 --> 00:36:29.077
So again, this is a
different white balance

00:36:29.077 --> 00:36:30.410
than the other side of the room.

00:36:33.770 --> 00:36:35.870
Now one thing that
Dave was talking about,

00:36:35.870 --> 00:36:40.670
we talked about camera angles
a lot on Wednesday night.

00:36:40.670 --> 00:36:44.030
We talked about Varta, we
talked about Nanook of the North

00:36:44.030 --> 00:36:46.164
and the way the camera
gets used in both of those.

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:52.190
And to go back to Dave
here for a minute,

00:36:52.190 --> 00:36:59.110
think about the difference
between this shot,

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:04.030
and this shot, and this shot.

00:37:09.850 --> 00:37:11.770
Giant Dave Stripinis.

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:14.560
Giant of film here.

00:37:14.560 --> 00:37:19.780
And then this is the
flare-outty, I know this guy,

00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:21.280
I'm going to I'm
going to learn more

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:23.260
about this guy kind of feeling.

00:37:23.260 --> 00:37:29.080
And this is the spy
cam, voyeuristic,

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:30.760
make Dave look
small kind of thing.

00:37:33.310 --> 00:37:35.560
So how you choose-- and
that's just one example--

00:37:35.560 --> 00:37:39.400
but how you choose
your shots throughout,

00:37:39.400 --> 00:37:43.330
and whether you're choosing
to be on a tripod or handheld,

00:37:43.330 --> 00:37:47.710
how you're moving the camera,
has an emotional resonance.

00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:51.410
It's very important.

