there are three options.

00:40:38.755 --> 00:40:40.630
You can put it all the
way to the back, which

00:40:40.630 --> 00:40:42.310
is manual focus.

00:40:42.310 --> 00:40:45.190
You can put it in the
middle, which is auto focus.

00:40:45.190 --> 00:40:49.550
So let's just assume, can
I use you for an example,

00:40:49.550 --> 00:40:53.230
[? Anna? ?] Let's
just zoom in here.

00:40:53.230 --> 00:40:57.960
And if I-- oh, that's right.

00:40:57.960 --> 00:41:00.510
Let me just mess with the focus.

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:05.290
Let's put it on auto
focus, and there you go.

00:41:05.290 --> 00:41:06.840
So if you're ever
in a situation,

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:09.300
like say you're
doing handheld stuff

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:12.000
and you're working hard
to follow somebody,

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:13.650
you may want to use auto focus.

00:41:13.650 --> 00:41:15.690
If you're staying
wide and you're

00:41:15.690 --> 00:41:20.170
following that person in
a relatively close manner.

00:41:20.170 --> 00:41:23.790
So in some instances it's good
to have, but for the most part

00:41:23.790 --> 00:41:25.560
I would recommend manual focus.

00:41:25.560 --> 00:41:26.850
CHRIS BOEBEL: Do you need
hat again for a wide shot--

00:41:26.850 --> 00:41:28.800
I got the detail of
your hands, and now I

00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:30.270
want to show the
screen, as well.

00:41:30.270 --> 00:41:32.670
DAVE STRIPINUS: Oh yeah, sure.

00:41:32.670 --> 00:41:37.890
So I'm going to put it on
manual focus and just mess

00:41:37.890 --> 00:41:38.950
up the focus.

00:41:38.950 --> 00:41:43.230
And once you switch
it to auto focus,

00:41:43.230 --> 00:41:47.550
it snaps right into focus.

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:54.570
At the front of this
three switch is zoom,

00:41:54.570 --> 00:41:56.850
and you can set
this ring right here

00:41:56.850 --> 00:42:01.020
to zoom if you push this
all the way to the front.

00:42:03.690 --> 00:42:08.750
And now this ring
acts as a zoom ring.

00:42:08.750 --> 00:42:14.960
So again, I would
keep this on focus,

00:42:14.960 --> 00:42:17.510
manual focus at the very back.

00:42:17.510 --> 00:42:19.980
On the right hand side
of the camera again,

00:42:19.980 --> 00:42:21.547
the assign button is right here.

00:42:21.547 --> 00:42:22.880
And this is for white balancing.

00:42:22.880 --> 00:42:25.640
So again, find a
white sheet of paper.

00:42:25.640 --> 00:42:27.230
If you're with
somebody else, just

00:42:27.230 --> 00:42:31.160
have them hold it up to whatever
your dominant light source is.

00:42:31.160 --> 00:42:35.210
Zoom in, try to fill the frame
about 80% with the white paper,

00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:38.570
press this assign button, and
it should white balance for you.

00:42:38.570 --> 00:42:40.700
You should see a little
blinking what looks

00:42:40.700 --> 00:42:43.232
like a flower in the LCD panel.

00:42:43.232 --> 00:42:44.690
When it's done
blinking, that means

00:42:44.690 --> 00:42:46.140
you're properly white balanced.

00:42:46.140 --> 00:42:47.890
And of course, you'll
see the color change

00:42:47.890 --> 00:42:49.470
in the actual LCD screen.

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:54.830
So let's see, other
auto features.

00:42:54.830 --> 00:42:57.410
You have to go into
the menu a little bit.

00:43:01.710 --> 00:43:05.880
Actually, I think you have to go
to the main menu for exposure.

00:43:05.880 --> 00:43:10.650
And you can't really
mirror it here.

00:43:10.650 --> 00:43:12.690
In the main menu, not
the personal menu,

00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:14.418
if you want to go
to auto exposure,

00:43:14.418 --> 00:43:16.710
which is a good idea if you're
leaving the camera alone

00:43:16.710 --> 00:43:21.870
and you're going to have a
lot of changes in lighting.

00:43:21.870 --> 00:43:24.450
If you go to camera set--

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:28.830
I don't if you can, this
might be a little weird--

00:43:28.830 --> 00:43:32.010
camera set in the main
menu, not the personal menu

00:43:32.010 --> 00:43:35.550
but the bigger menu.

00:43:35.550 --> 00:43:42.600
And you go to exposure, you
have a choice of auto or manual.

00:43:42.600 --> 00:43:45.240
And if you're just going to
set the camera up on a tripod--

00:43:45.240 --> 00:43:46.950
and again, it's
going to be changes

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:49.170
in the lighting in the
room, I would go with auto.

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:55.670
What else?

00:43:55.670 --> 00:43:57.670
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, in
the second half of class,

00:43:57.670 --> 00:44:00.100
everyone is going to get the
sound package and the camera

00:44:00.100 --> 00:44:02.620
package, and practice
working with them together.

00:44:02.620 --> 00:44:04.660
And that also might
be a good time for you

00:44:04.660 --> 00:44:07.510
to just look at some
of these settings

00:44:07.510 --> 00:44:09.460
again, and talk to
either Dave or I

00:44:09.460 --> 00:44:13.660
if you have questions
or aren't sure about how

00:44:13.660 --> 00:44:15.470
to get from one kind
of setting to another,

00:44:15.470 --> 00:44:20.988
or how to do focus and
exposure, a white balance.

00:44:20.988 --> 00:44:22.780
It's going to take a
little bit of practice

00:44:22.780 --> 00:44:26.080
until it becomes kind
of second nature.

00:44:26.080 --> 00:44:28.450
So before we move
over to sound--

00:44:28.450 --> 00:44:30.800
and we'll take a quick
break before we do that--

00:44:30.800 --> 00:44:34.855
I want to talk a little bit more
about putting shots together,

00:44:34.855 --> 00:44:36.730
what we've been kind of
calling film grammar.

00:44:46.210 --> 00:44:49.570
So thinking back to the films
that we looked at on Wednesday

00:44:49.570 --> 00:44:55.330
night, what kinds of
shots were they using?

00:44:55.330 --> 00:44:59.956
Give me some ideas of the
kinds of things we saw.

00:44:59.956 --> 00:45:05.343
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:05.343 --> 00:45:07.510
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right, so
wide shots, we'll call them.

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:14.260
What else?

00:45:17.250 --> 00:45:18.803
AUDIENCE: Like some close-ups.

00:45:18.803 --> 00:45:20.220
CHRIS BOEBEL:
Close-ups of people.

00:45:26.810 --> 00:45:27.370
What else?

00:45:31.274 --> 00:45:32.545
AUDIENCE: Medium shots.

00:45:32.545 --> 00:45:33.670
CHRIS BOEBEL: Medium shots.

00:45:33.670 --> 00:45:35.950
Right, which is kind
of the in-between.

00:45:35.950 --> 00:45:50.455
So what about Varta, how was he
using the camera differently?

00:45:50.455 --> 00:45:54.090
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:54.090 --> 00:45:56.580
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right,
extreme angles.

00:45:56.580 --> 00:45:58.690
Any of these could also
be an extreme angle,

00:45:58.690 --> 00:46:00.660
but that's definitely
worth putting up here.

00:46:10.350 --> 00:46:15.983
In Nanook, there's the
scene with the fishing.

00:46:15.983 --> 00:46:18.150
What other kinds of shots
did you see in that scene,

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:19.970
if you remember?

00:46:19.970 --> 00:46:23.717
AUDIENCE: Back and forth
between far away and zoomed in.

00:46:23.717 --> 00:46:24.550
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right.

00:46:24.550 --> 00:46:27.730
Now, it was also zooming in--
and I don't think actually

00:46:27.730 --> 00:46:29.410
zoomed, but it's like--

00:46:29.410 --> 00:46:31.990
AUDIENCE: It was like focus
on the fish, and then--

00:46:31.990 --> 00:46:34.330
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yes, so
focusing on the fish

00:46:34.330 --> 00:46:36.220
is a different kind of shot.

00:46:36.220 --> 00:46:40.540
And that has different names,
but what I'll call it for now

00:46:40.540 --> 00:46:44.290
is what's often referred
to as the insert shot.

00:46:44.290 --> 00:46:46.630
And what that means
is you insert it

00:46:46.630 --> 00:46:49.670
in the middle of the action to
show a close-up of an activity.

00:46:49.670 --> 00:46:56.410
So for instance, the shot of
paying for the food in the line

00:46:56.410 --> 00:46:58.420
is kind of an insert shot.

00:46:58.420 --> 00:47:01.930
And it's definitely
worth putting up here.

00:47:01.930 --> 00:47:04.490
DAVE STRIPINUS: Do you want
to tie that into coverage?

00:47:04.490 --> 00:47:06.032
CHRIS BOEBEL: That's
where I'm going.

00:47:06.032 --> 00:47:08.020
Yeah.

00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:11.270
So anything else?

00:47:11.270 --> 00:47:13.150
Think about Varta.

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:18.460
Think about the shots of
the people in the carriage,

00:47:18.460 --> 00:47:20.182
the train's--

00:47:20.182 --> 00:47:20.890
AUDIENCE: Moving.

00:47:20.890 --> 00:47:21.220
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right.

00:47:21.220 --> 00:47:21.835
Moving shots.

00:47:30.130 --> 00:47:35.470
And that could be handheld,
like we were doing,

00:47:35.470 --> 00:47:37.000
or it could be a fixed shot.

00:47:37.000 --> 00:47:39.010
It could be what we call
a dolly shot, except we

00:47:39.010 --> 00:47:40.540
don't have a dolly.

00:47:40.540 --> 00:47:44.740
But if you could be mounted
in a car, as Varta was.

00:47:44.740 --> 00:47:48.620
You could be mounted on
a train or a streetcar.

00:47:48.620 --> 00:47:54.945
The generic term for that might
be a dolly shot, with the idea

00:47:54.945 --> 00:47:57.070
being that would be smoother
than a hand-held shot,

00:47:57.070 --> 00:47:59.528
because you're on some kind of
wheeled vehicle on a tripod,

00:47:59.528 --> 00:48:01.300
probably.

00:48:01.300 --> 00:48:05.230
So this is not at all a
mutually exclusive list.

00:48:05.230 --> 00:48:07.090
I mean, a moving shot
could be a wide shot.

00:48:07.090 --> 00:48:07.900
It could be a close-up.

00:48:07.900 --> 00:48:08.983
It could be a medium shot.

00:48:08.983 --> 00:48:10.930
It could even be an insert shot.

00:48:10.930 --> 00:48:13.480
Extreme angles, could
be any of these.

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:16.150
Handheld, you could have
a handheld wide shot,

00:48:16.150 --> 00:48:17.080
a handheld close-up.

00:48:17.080 --> 00:48:21.100
So this is not an
exclusive list,

00:48:21.100 --> 00:48:27.190
but if you look at these, wide
shot, close-up, medium shot,

00:48:27.190 --> 00:48:29.260
insert shot, that's
going to cover

00:48:29.260 --> 00:48:32.650
a lot of the kind of thing that
you're going to be shooting.

00:48:32.650 --> 00:48:38.680
And they fit together in very
specific and particular ways.

00:48:38.680 --> 00:48:42.790
And that's what we really
refer to as film grammar.

00:48:42.790 --> 00:48:46.600
And these other things, as
I said, all of these things

00:48:46.600 --> 00:48:49.270
could be one of these
or anything else.

00:48:54.290 --> 00:48:56.770
So I'll pick up the
camera in a minute.

00:48:56.770 --> 00:49:02.050
But let's say we're filming a
scene of two people talking,

00:49:02.050 --> 00:49:07.340
and I'm drawing
it from overhead.

00:49:07.340 --> 00:49:08.450
These are their noses.

00:49:08.450 --> 00:49:08.950
OK.

00:49:13.600 --> 00:49:16.810
So how might we
shoot that scene?

00:49:21.410 --> 00:49:27.487
AUDIENCE: Just stay on one
side and the [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:27.487 --> 00:49:28.320
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right.

00:49:28.320 --> 00:49:32.360
So what you're referring
to is what is typically

00:49:32.360 --> 00:49:33.440
referred to as the line.

00:49:33.440 --> 00:49:40.172
And this is kind of a basic
concept in filmmaking, which

00:49:40.172 --> 00:49:42.380
sounds like you're familiar
with, but the rest of you

00:49:42.380 --> 00:49:43.430
may not be.

00:49:43.430 --> 00:49:45.710
The idea of the line
is that whatever scene

00:49:45.710 --> 00:49:48.590
you're shooting, whether it's
a simple scene of two people,

00:49:48.590 --> 00:49:52.040
or even one person, or a
more complicated scene,

00:49:52.040 --> 00:49:56.420
you draw an imaginary line
through the center of it

00:49:56.420 --> 00:49:58.100
like that.

00:49:58.100 --> 00:50:02.000
And that axis gives you
a frame of reference

00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:02.930
to shoot the scene.

00:50:02.930 --> 00:50:07.100
And as long as you stay on one
side or the other of the line,

00:50:07.100 --> 00:50:09.470
the shots that you
shoot make sense

00:50:09.470 --> 00:50:11.360
when they are put together.

00:50:11.360 --> 00:50:13.830
That's kind of in editing.

00:50:13.830 --> 00:50:20.060
So what I mean by that is,
let's say we come down here,

00:50:20.060 --> 00:50:24.422
if you can see that, and film
a wide shot from the side

00:50:24.422 --> 00:50:25.505
of the two people talking.

00:50:28.980 --> 00:50:35.393
So if we do that, person
one is looking camera right,

00:50:35.393 --> 00:50:37.310
they're looking to the
right side of the lens,

00:50:37.310 --> 00:50:39.690
and person two is
looking to the left.

00:50:39.690 --> 00:50:42.740
Now if I put the camera
anywhere else along that axis,

00:50:42.740 --> 00:50:45.140
or along the side
of the axis, they're

00:50:45.140 --> 00:50:47.610
still going to be looking
the same direction.

00:50:47.610 --> 00:50:53.030
So I could come over here
and film over their shoulder,

00:50:53.030 --> 00:50:54.800
and they're still
looking camera right.

00:50:54.800 --> 00:50:55.880
Less so, but still.

00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:57.770
And they're still
looking camera left.

00:50:57.770 --> 00:51:01.130
I could come over here
and do the same thing.

00:51:01.130 --> 00:51:03.410
Can shoot close-ups, I
can shoot wide shots.

00:51:03.410 --> 00:51:07.610
If they were working
at something together,

00:51:07.610 --> 00:51:09.590
I could shoot insert
shots of whatever

00:51:09.590 --> 00:51:11.360
it is that they're doing.

00:51:11.360 --> 00:51:15.110
And the hands would still be
all oriented the same way.

00:51:15.110 --> 00:51:18.110
Person one is always
oriented to the right,

00:51:18.110 --> 00:51:20.340
and person to is
oriented to the left.

00:51:20.340 --> 00:51:23.210
But if I come over here, if
I cross the line and start

00:51:23.210 --> 00:51:25.080
shooting on this
side, all of a sudden,

00:51:25.080 --> 00:51:27.470
it's completely flipped.

00:51:27.470 --> 00:51:30.200
Person one is now
looking to the left.

00:51:30.200 --> 00:51:36.290
And in editing, the
standard form of editing

00:51:36.290 --> 00:51:40.263
is that that is considered
jarring or confusing.

00:51:40.263 --> 00:51:41.930
It doesn't flow as
smoothly if you start

00:51:41.930 --> 00:51:43.340
cutting those shots together.

00:51:43.340 --> 00:51:48.470
Now in 2011, those rules are
really not hard and fast rules.

00:51:48.470 --> 00:51:52.960
I mean, Quentin Tarantino
started crossing the line--

00:51:52.960 --> 00:51:54.180
well, he wasn't the first.

00:51:54.180 --> 00:51:57.830
But at this point in
terms of filmmaking,

00:51:57.830 --> 00:51:59.480
we accept all sorts
of things, and we

00:51:59.480 --> 00:52:01.310
don't worry as much about it.

00:52:01.310 --> 00:52:03.350
But when you're starting
out figuring out

00:52:03.350 --> 00:52:05.180
how to shoot
scenes, I think it's

00:52:05.180 --> 00:52:08.270
important to know these
rules and then later learn

00:52:08.270 --> 00:52:10.650
how to break them.

00:52:10.650 --> 00:52:12.650
So let's just quickly--

00:52:12.650 --> 00:52:15.143
can we set the camera up?

00:52:15.143 --> 00:52:16.310
Let me show you what I mean.

00:52:16.310 --> 00:52:17.185
It's probably easier.

00:52:44.450 --> 00:52:47.640
So Dennis, I'm
blocking you, right?

00:52:47.640 --> 00:52:48.726
AUDIENCE: Right now, yeah.

00:52:52.820 --> 00:52:55.860
CHRIS BOEBEL: Come over here.

00:52:55.860 --> 00:52:58.860
OK, so Dave and
[INAUDIBLE],, can I

00:52:58.860 --> 00:53:00.930
have guys do our little scene?

00:53:00.930 --> 00:53:03.152
Let's say you're talking.

00:53:03.152 --> 00:53:04.860
And I'm going to come
around here, simply

00:53:04.860 --> 00:53:07.810
because if you could
just orient yourselves

00:53:07.810 --> 00:53:09.270
so the window is not behind you.

00:53:09.270 --> 00:53:18.352
Just-- great.

00:53:18.352 --> 00:53:19.560
Dave, a little more this way.

00:53:19.560 --> 00:53:22.210
Yeah.

00:53:22.210 --> 00:53:24.290
DAVE STRIPINUS: See
any good movies lately?

00:53:24.290 --> 00:53:26.680
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:53:26.680 --> 00:53:29.775
DAVE STRIPINUS: Which ones?

00:53:29.775 --> 00:53:31.900
CHRIS BOEBEL: OK, so you
see Dave is looking camera

00:53:31.900 --> 00:53:34.570
left, and she's
looking camera right.

00:53:38.850 --> 00:53:43.215
Dave is looking camera left,
she's looking camera right.

00:53:43.215 --> 00:53:44.590
She's still looking
camera right.

00:53:47.730 --> 00:53:50.300
I live

00:53:50.300 --> 00:53:53.300
DAVE STRIPINUS: Another Year
is playing at the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:53:53.300 --> 00:53:57.640
CHRIS BOEBEL: Dave is
looking camera left.

00:53:57.640 --> 00:53:59.770
Dave is looking camera left.

00:53:59.770 --> 00:54:03.670
And if they were
actually working together

00:54:03.670 --> 00:54:07.450
on some kind of activity,
they would still

00:54:07.450 --> 00:54:11.980
be looking the same way.

00:54:11.980 --> 00:54:14.290
So my line, it goes through
the center of the scene,

00:54:14.290 --> 00:54:16.090
just as we described.

00:54:16.090 --> 00:54:19.600
If I come over here,
all of a sudden,

00:54:19.600 --> 00:54:22.570
suddenly she's
looking camera left.

00:54:22.570 --> 00:54:24.110
Because I crossed the line.

00:54:24.110 --> 00:54:25.527
DAVE STRIPINUS:
Oh, I missed that.

00:54:25.527 --> 00:54:26.110
Yeah.

00:54:26.110 --> 00:54:28.360
CHRIS BOEBEL: So that's the
basic concept of the line.

00:54:28.360 --> 00:54:32.020
And it can get confusing if you
have a very complicated scene.

00:54:32.020 --> 00:54:34.930
Like again, imagine
this classroom.

00:54:34.930 --> 00:54:38.770
Imagine somebody up here talking
and the rest of you listening.

00:54:38.770 --> 00:54:41.600
If I stay on this
side of the classroom,

00:54:41.600 --> 00:54:43.570
this person is always
looking to the right,

00:54:43.570 --> 00:54:45.850
and everyone here is
looking to the left.

00:54:45.850 --> 00:54:48.200
Dennis is shooting to the left.

00:54:48.200 --> 00:54:51.220
But if I go over there, all
of a sudden it's reversed.

00:54:51.220 --> 00:54:54.640
So that's kind of the basic
concept of film grammar.

00:54:54.640 --> 00:54:58.690
And as I said, it's
broken all the time.

00:54:58.690 --> 00:55:01.000
Sometimes intentionally,
sometimes unintentionally.

00:55:01.000 --> 00:55:03.760
And people have gotten a lot
more used to reading scenes

00:55:03.760 --> 00:55:04.780
when it's broken.

00:55:04.780 --> 00:55:07.210
But it's kind of
still a basic thing

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:09.010
that you want to
get familiar with

00:55:09.010 --> 00:55:15.610
and you want to use to film
your basic scenes before you

00:55:15.610 --> 00:55:22.000
understand how to not do it,
or when it's good to not do it.

00:55:22.000 --> 00:55:24.880
One example of when you
might want to change

00:55:24.880 --> 00:55:32.020
would be to change
the feel of a space,

00:55:32.020 --> 00:55:34.310
if you're shooting the
same space for a long time.

00:55:34.310 --> 00:55:38.050
So for instance, if I was doing
a documentary on this class,

00:55:38.050 --> 00:55:42.400
and I was in here for
three hours, at some point

00:55:42.400 --> 00:55:46.480
I might change on purpose
so that I create a kind

00:55:46.480 --> 00:55:48.530
of a new feeling in the class.

00:55:48.530 --> 00:55:51.550
So maybe I would
film the camera part

00:55:51.550 --> 00:55:56.140
looking this way with the film
grammar set up on this axis.

00:55:56.140 --> 00:55:57.880
And then maybe when
we switch to sound,

00:55:57.880 --> 00:55:59.560
I go over there
and film this way.

00:55:59.560 --> 00:56:03.190
Although these windows
might dictate my choice.

00:56:03.190 --> 00:56:05.410
This would be a very
difficult background.

00:56:05.410 --> 00:56:10.660
But in other words, you can
change and you can switch,

00:56:10.660 --> 00:56:14.200
but that's also a situation
in which I probably

00:56:14.200 --> 00:56:16.540
wouldn't be intercutting
the first stuff that I

00:56:16.540 --> 00:56:19.490
shot with the second
stuff that I shot.

00:56:19.490 --> 00:56:26.560
So it's just a way of
helping your audience just

00:56:26.560 --> 00:56:29.530
feel comfortable in the scene,
understand where people are,

00:56:29.530 --> 00:56:31.910
accept everything.

00:56:31.910 --> 00:56:34.900
And although there are
plenty of times that you may

00:56:34.900 --> 00:56:38.050
want to intentionally break it.

00:56:38.050 --> 00:56:40.840
So did that make sense?

00:56:40.840 --> 00:56:41.620
Questions?

00:56:46.280 --> 00:56:48.620
Want to add anything?

00:56:48.620 --> 00:56:50.320
DAVE STRIPINUS: No,
I think that's good.

00:56:55.840 --> 00:56:56.623
CHRIS BOEBEL: OK.

00:56:56.623 --> 00:56:58.540
The only other thing
that I'll talk about just

00:56:58.540 --> 00:57:00.760
quickly is focal length.

00:57:00.760 --> 00:57:03.310
I mean, I talked a bit
about that voyeuristic

00:57:03.310 --> 00:57:06.730
feel that you get
with a long lens.

00:57:06.730 --> 00:57:09.050
That is one issue
to think about.

00:57:09.050 --> 00:57:14.170
And again, remember that the
focal length that you shoot on

00:57:14.170 --> 00:57:16.600
is not the same as
the size of the shot.

00:57:16.600 --> 00:57:20.230
So I'm right now shooting
as wide as I possibly can.

00:57:24.910 --> 00:57:27.940
And now I'm shooting
a close-up, because I

00:57:27.940 --> 00:57:30.980
went to a very long lens.

00:57:30.980 --> 00:57:32.610
DAVE STRIPINUS: Is
the study shot on?

00:57:32.610 --> 00:57:33.902
CHRIS BOEBEL: Oh, probably not.

00:57:33.902 --> 00:57:34.970
No, it's not.

00:57:34.970 --> 00:57:35.600
That's OK.

00:57:39.100 --> 00:57:43.480
However, I can also
shoot a close-up

00:57:43.480 --> 00:57:46.585
on a very wide lens, which
will have a different feel.

00:57:49.700 --> 00:57:54.020
For one thing, it's smoother
if you're shooting handheld,

00:57:54.020 --> 00:57:59.120
but it also tends to
change people's faces.

00:57:59.120 --> 00:58:03.650
Long lenses are actually very
flattering to the human face.

00:58:03.650 --> 00:58:06.650
So if you want to
make people look good,

00:58:06.650 --> 00:58:09.625
shooting on a longer
lens is a good idea.

00:58:09.625 --> 00:58:11.750
It also changes the
relationship between foreground

00:58:11.750 --> 00:58:12.375
and background.

00:58:12.375 --> 00:58:14.240
You see all of a
sudden the background

00:58:14.240 --> 00:58:16.040
is completely in focus?

00:58:16.040 --> 00:58:21.680
Which means that we are less
likely to pick out the face.

00:58:21.680 --> 00:58:32.490
If I come back here and
go back to a long lens,

00:58:32.490 --> 00:58:35.970
the background
starts to get soft.

00:58:35.970 --> 00:58:37.800
And you can see how
shaky it is, too.

00:58:37.800 --> 00:58:39.180
The steady shot isn't on either.

00:58:41.728 --> 00:58:43.270
Now I don't have
enough room in here,

00:58:43.270 --> 00:58:45.640
but the other thing that
you can do, of course,

00:58:45.640 --> 00:58:48.090
is have a wide shot
that's on a long lens.

00:58:48.090 --> 00:58:51.540
If I got back
another 50 yards, I

00:58:51.540 --> 00:58:56.460
could have a nice wide shot of
this room on the farthest, most

00:58:56.460 --> 00:58:58.950
telephoto focal length.

00:58:58.950 --> 00:59:01.358
And that is when you
start to get into kind

00:59:01.358 --> 00:59:02.400
of voyeuristic territory.

00:59:02.400 --> 00:59:04.170
I mean, think again
about that shot

00:59:04.170 --> 00:59:06.570
through the bars of the Kissing.

00:59:06.570 --> 00:59:12.270
That was shot-- it's a widish
shot shot on a long lens.

00:59:12.270 --> 00:59:14.680
But again, your depth of
field is very shallow.

00:59:14.680 --> 00:59:17.700
So you can do things like
rack focus in and out.

00:59:17.700 --> 00:59:21.000
It can be very, very
beautiful way to shoot things,

00:59:21.000 --> 00:59:27.940
but it has a particular
emotional kind of feeling.

00:59:27.940 --> 00:59:32.170
So thoughts, questions about
this stuff before we take

00:59:32.170 --> 00:59:33.490
a break and switch into sound?

00:59:38.633 --> 00:59:41.216
AUDIENCE: Are you also using a
long lens when using long lens?

00:59:43.970 --> 00:59:45.320
CHRIS BOEBEL: But zoomed in.

00:59:45.320 --> 00:59:47.840
And this camera
zoomed all the way in,

00:59:47.840 --> 00:59:52.490
the longest focal length,
meaning the field of view

00:59:52.490 --> 00:59:55.280
of the lenses, as narrow
as it can possibly be.

01:00:00.490 --> 01:00:03.700
Right, well so let's
take a 5-minute break.

01:00:03.700 --> 01:00:05.740
And then when we
come back, we'll

01:00:05.740 --> 01:00:09.310
do a quick introduction to audio
recording and an introduction

01:00:09.310 --> 01:00:10.570
to our audio package.

01:00:10.570 --> 01:00:13.360
And then we'll get a chance to
spend some time playing around

01:00:13.360 --> 01:00:16.440
with both the camera
and the audio gear.

